steps.body:
A *pipeline* chains goroutines with channels. Each stage takes a `<-chan T`, starts a goroutine, and returns a new `<-chan T` with its results.

```go
func square(in <-chan int) <-chan int {
	out := make(chan int)
	go func() {
		defer close(out)
		for n := range in {
			out <- n * n
		}
	}()
	return out
}
```

Because each stage ranges over its input and closes its output when that loop ends, closing the first channel shuts the whole pipeline down in turn. Nested calls read inside out. If `gen` is a first stage that sends its arguments on a new channel, `square(gen(1, 2, 3))` feeds `gen` into `square`.

A stage does not have to send one value per input. A filter stage sends only the values that pass a test.

Each stage handles its values one at a time in one goroutine, so results come out in the same order the numbers went in. All stages run at the same time: while `square` works on 2, `gen` can already be sending 3. With unbuffered channels only a few values are in flight at once, however long the input is.

Read the final channel until it closes. If you stop early, the stages block on their next send and never finish.

